<div class="modal fade" id="level11Modal" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="clinicHealthCentreModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-dialog-scrollable" style="max-width:800px;">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
         <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
            <img src="https://id.concordreview.com/wp-content/plugins/w2gm/resources/images/map_icons/icons/_new/hospital_pin-green.png" style="width:30px; height:30px;">
            <h5 class="modal-title" id="clinicHealthCentreModalLabel">Clinic / Health Centre</h5>
         </div>
        <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="modal" aria-label="Close"></button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
        <p class="p-modal text-justify" data-modal-section="overview">
            Area Health Centers (AHCs) are the main primary-care facilities with inpatient capacity. They correspond to the statutory &ldquo;health center,&rdquo; defined as premises with some residential accommodation that provide and organize integrated primary health care for a defined area and have &ldquo;supervisory and referral duties from clinics in its area&rdquo; (Health Services Act, Section 2). The RDP splits them into two levels. AHC Level 1 serves a population of 1,000&ndash;2,500; AHC Level 2 serves 2,500 or more, or 1,000&ndash;4,000 where access to a General Hospital is restricted. There is generally one AHC in each health zone of each province, and each supervises at least three Rural Health Centers. In towns, Urban Health Centers play an equivalent role: UHC-1 is an outpatient clinic in Honiara or a provincial capital, and UHC-2 is a larger Honiara center that can admit patients for up to 48 hours.
        </p>
        <p class="p-modal text-justify">
            <strong>Note:</strong> WHO&rsquo;s 2014 profile counted 37 Area Health Centers. Before the RDP, AHCs were grouped as small, medium or large; they have since been reorganized into AHC1 and AHC2. Urban Health Centers do not have a separate statutory category and fall under the Act&rsquo;s &ldquo;health center&rdquo; or &ldquo;clinic&rdquo; definitions depending on size. Record AHCs as &ldquo;Area Health Center (AHC1/AHC2)&rdquo; and urban centers as &ldquo;Urban Health Center (UHC-1/UHC-2)&rdquo; where the source says which; otherwise record simply &ldquo;Area Health Center&rdquo; or &ldquo;Urban Health Center.&rdquo;
        </p>
        <ul data-modal-section="role">
            <li>Provide general outpatient, dental and cancer screening services and act as the triage and referral point to the provincial hospital</li>
            <li>Supervise the Rural Health Centers in their zone; Zone Health Managers are based at AHCs</li>
            <li>AHC2 adds accident and emergency care, minor procedures, psychiatric emergencies and coordination of patient transfers.</li>
        </ul>
        <h6 class="fw-bold" data-modal-section="services">Typical Bed Capacity</h6>
        <ul>
            <li>No bed number set; the RDP sets minimum activity instead: AHC1 at least 100 admissions a year (stays of 3&ndash;5 days) and AHC2 at least 200 admissions a year (stays of 5&ndash;7 days). UHC-1 has no inpatient service; UHC-2 allows short admissions of 24&ndash;48 hours.</li>
        </ul>
        <h6 class="fw-bold">Diagnostic &amp; Support Infrastructure</h6>
        <ul>
            <li>AHC1: at least three registered nurses or nurse aides with midwifery training, a public health officer, and a visiting or resident medical officer and laboratory staff</li>
            <li>AHC2: indicatively a full-time resident medical officer, pharmacist, laboratory technician and dental staff</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="p-modal text-justify">
            <em><strong>Note:</strong> Legal basis: Health Services Act (Cap. 100), Sections 2 and 11. Levels, catchments and staffing come from the MHMS Role Delineation Policy; the facility count comes from WHO (2014). These details are not confirmed against the Act itself.</em>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
